WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.172 --> 00:00:09.755
<v Maya>If I told you that back in the late 1800s, machines basically wiped out nearly half of all farming jobs, you'd probably assume the economy just completely collapsed. Right,</v>

2
00:00:09.835 --> 00:00:10.275
<v Daniel>which it didn't.</v>

3
00:00:10.675 --> 00:00:20.637
<v Maya>Exactly. It didn't. It actually boomed. And today, we're all staring down generative AI, and there's this massive collective anxiety that robots are going to take all our jobs.</v>

4
00:00:20.778 --> 00:00:21.618
<v Daniel>It's everywhere, yeah.</v>

5
00:00:21.798 --> 00:00:32.403
<v Maya>But the actual math we're looking at today suggests something, well, totally counterintuitive. We shouldn't actually fear brilliant AI. We should be absolutely terrified of mediocre AI.</v>

6
00:00:32.563 --> 00:00:46.311
<v Daniel>I know. It is a wild paradox. When you look at the historical data, the true danger to the human worker isn't some super intelligent machine. The real danger is a piece of technology that is just barely smart enough to replace you, but</v>

7
00:00:47.692 --> 00:00:49.953
<v Daniel>not smart enough to actually make the economy any richer.</v>

8
00:00:50.392 --> 00:00:55.335
<v Maya>And that is exactly what we're getting into today. So welcome to the Steep Dive, custom tailored just for you.</v>

9
00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:56.215
<v Daniel>Glad to be here.</v>

10
00:00:56.936 --> 00:01:07.762
<v Maya>Our mission today is to cut through all that endless, anxious noise about the future of work and answer one fundamental question. Does technology create new work or does it only take old work?</v>

11
00:01:07.982 --> 00:01:09.183
<v Daniel>It's the million dollar question.</v>

12
00:01:09.487 --> 00:01:21.591
<v Maya>Right. And to do this, we're unpacking a pretty heavy-hitting stack of economic research. Our anchor is this classic, really paradigm-shifting 2019 paper by Daron Esamoglu and Pascual Restrepo.</v>

13
00:01:22.131 --> 00:01:23.191
<v Daniel>Automation and New Tasks.</v>

14
00:01:23.491 --> 00:01:35.435
<v Maya>Yes, that's the one. And then we're going to stress test their theory against three major follow-up papers from between 2022 and 2025 to see, you know, how the math actually holds up in the age of chat GPT.</v>

15
00:01:36.001 --> 00:01:47.502
<v Daniel>It is an incredibly rich stack of evidence. And if you're trying to understand the actual mechanics of labor demand rather than just, you know, sci-fi speculation, this is the foundational framework you need.</v>

16
00:01:47.804 --> 00:01:56.749
<v Maya>Okay, let's unpack this because most of the conventional wisdom we hear about AI and jobs is actually relying on a totally outdated textbook economic model.</v>

17
00:01:57.549 --> 00:01:57.830
<v Daniel>Totally.</v>

18
00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:07.715
<v Maya>And the 2019 paper argues we literally have to dismantle that model before we can understand anything else. So how did the old textbooks define technology's impact on work?</v>

19
00:02:08.246 --> 00:02:13.051
<v Daniel>So the old textbook view is what economists call the factor augmenting view.</v>

20
00:02:13.591 --> 00:02:13.851
<v Maya>Okay.</v>

21
00:02:13.931 --> 00:02:21.919
<v Daniel>It essentially assumes that a new technology simply makes capital, like machinery, or human labor, uniformly more productive across the board.</v>

22
00:02:22.299 --> 00:02:23.480
<v Maya>So it's just a multiplier.</v>

23
00:02:23.681 --> 00:02:31.468
<v Daniel>Exactly. In that model, technology just scales up what humans can already do, which theoretically means everybody's paycheck eventually gets a little bigger.</v>

24
00:02:31.925 --> 00:02:46.880
<v Maya>Right. A rising tide lifts all boats kind of thing. But Osamoglu and Restrepo introduced something entirely different, which they called the task-based framework. Yes. They argue that production isn't just one big monolith. It's a series of individual tasks. Let me</v>

25
00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.062
<v Maya>try an analogy here just to see if I've got this right.</v>

26
00:02:49.142 --> 00:02:49.483
<v Daniel>Go for it.</v>

27
00:02:49.903 --> 00:03:04.811
<v Maya>So think about building a house. The old textbook theory says a new better hammer just makes the human carpenter uniformly faster at building the entire house. Right. But the task based framework says, no, that's not how automation works at all. What actually happens is a</v>

28
00:03:04.871 --> 00:03:19.459
<v Maya>robot takes over the specific task of laying bricks. So the human is entirely displaced from bricklaying. However, humans then invent an entirely new task like, say, installing smart home networking, which keeps a human on the construction site just</v>

29
00:03:19.839 --> 00:03:21.060
<v Maya>doing something entirely different.</v>

30
00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:24.942
<v Daniel>That analogy hits the nail on the head. Literally.</v>

31
00:03:25.202 --> 00:03:25.522
<v Maya>Nice one.</v>

32
00:03:26.142 --> 00:03:35.267
<v Daniel>In this framework, automation, which they specifically define as an increase in capital-intensive tasks, literally rips tasks away from humans and gives them to machines.</v>

33
00:03:35.447 --> 00:03:35.987
<v Maya>Wow, okay.</v>

34
00:03:36.427 --> 00:03:47.045
<v Daniel>But conversely, the creation of new labor-intensive tasks moves work back toward humans. So the economy is basically this ongoing massive tug of war for the task content of production.</v>

35
00:03:47.725 --> 00:03:57.894
<v Maya>OK, so if technology is just passing tasks back and forth between humans and machines, what actually determines whether workers win or lose the tug of war in the end?</v>

36
00:03:58.175 --> 00:04:04.580
<v Daniel>Well, the 2019 paper breaks this down into four distinct forces of technological change. First, you have the displacement effect.</v>

37
00:04:04.901 --> 00:04:06.102
<v Maya>Which sounds bad.</v>

38
00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:18.064
<v Daniel>It is, for the worker. By definition, automation always lowers the labor share because it takes a specific task away from a human. Think of like a self-checkout kiosk at the grocery store, displacing the human cashier.</v>

39
00:04:18.284 --> 00:04:19.145
<v Maya>Right, makes sense.</v>

40
00:04:20.105 --> 00:04:27.548
<v Daniel>Second, you have the productivity effect. Technology makes those groceries cheaper to process and sell, which grows the overall economic pie.</v>

41
00:04:27.928 --> 00:04:35.911
<v Maya>Okay, because if my groceries are cheaper, I have more money left in my pocket to spend on going out to a restaurant, which theoretically creates labor demand over there.</v>

42
00:04:36.338 --> 00:04:42.644
<v Daniel>Precisely the mechanism. Now, the third force is the reinstatement effect. This is the holy grail.</v>

43
00:04:42.844 --> 00:04:43.485
<v Maya>The new tasks.</v>

44
00:04:43.905 --> 00:04:50.792
<v Daniel>Exactly. This is the creation of brand new tasks, which raises labor demand and violently shifts the labor share back toward humans.</v>

45
00:04:51.077 --> 00:04:53.738
<v Maya>So that's the smart home installer from my analogy.</v>

46
00:04:53.858 --> 00:05:01.822
<v Daniel>Right. Or the software engineers needed to design the self-checkout system or data analysts who optimize the store layout based on the kiosk data.</v>

47
00:05:01.942 --> 00:05:02.262
<v Maya>Got it.</v>

48
00:05:02.862 --> 00:05:11.226
<v Daniel>And then fourth is the composition effect, which is simply the reallocation of wealth and jobs across different sectors of the economy as those relative prices change.</v>

49
00:05:11.566 --> 00:05:17.488
<v Maya>Okay. This is where we get back to that paradox I mentioned at the very beginning, the so-so technology argument.</v>

50
00:05:17.749 --> 00:05:18.649
<v Daniel>Oh, this is fascinating.</v>

51
00:05:19.008 --> 00:05:28.454
<v Maya>Yeah, the paper says that brilliant technology isn't the real threat. Mediocre automation is. And they use automated customer service phone trees as the perfect example.</v>

52
00:05:28.754 --> 00:05:29.534
<v Daniel>We all hate those.</v>

53
00:05:29.895 --> 00:05:39.640
<v Maya>Right. They displace the human call center worker, so the human loses the job. But the tech is so annoying and terrible at resolving issues that it offers virtually zero productivity gains to the company.</v>

54
00:05:39.801 --> 00:05:41.982
<v Daniel>It doesn't actually make anything cheaper or better.</v>

55
00:05:42.462 --> 00:05:46.925
<v Maya>Exactly. It doesn't grow the economic pie enough to create new jobs anywhere else.</v>

56
00:05:47.716 --> 00:05:55.704
<v Daniel>It is literally the worst of both worlds. SoSo technology brings all of the displacement effect and none of the productivity effect.</v>

57
00:05:56.464 --> 00:05:56.625
<v Maya>Ouch.</v>

58
00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:07.546
<v Daniel>And this ties into a classic economic concept called the Habakkuk hypothesis. Historically, automation actually pays off the most when human labor is scarce and wages are incredibly high.</v>

59
00:06:07.806 --> 00:06:08.126
<v Maya>Really?</v>

60
00:06:08.346 --> 00:06:18.011
<v Daniel>Yeah. It explains why automation has been highly successful and far less destructive to workers in countries with aging populations like Germany, Japan, and South Korea compared to the U.S.</v>

61
00:06:18.391 --> 00:06:19.632
<v Maya>Oh, because they have to automate.</v>

62
00:06:19.953 --> 00:06:26.460
<v Daniel>Right. When a company is forced to pay a very high wage, they only invest in truly brilliant, highly productive automation.</v>

63
00:06:27.021 --> 00:06:27.301
<v Maya>I see.</v>

64
00:06:27.541 --> 00:06:35.710
<v Daniel>But in a low-wage environment, companies are tempted to buy so-so tech just to marginally undercut cheap human labor, which just stagnates the whole economy.</v>

65
00:06:36.023 --> 00:06:44.978
<v Maya>Wait, so we want machines to be incredibly brilliant to save our jobs. That feels totally counterintuitive. I mean, I would have thought super smart AI is what we should fear most.</v>

66
00:06:45.218 --> 00:06:50.627
<v Daniel>It does feel backward, but we need the technology to be brilliant so that the productivity effect is massive.</v>

67
00:06:51.427 --> 00:06:58.592
<v Maya>Ah, because a massive productivity effect generates the wealth necessary to fund the invention of new human tasks. It drives the reinstatement effect.</v>

68
00:06:58.732 --> 00:07:09.619
<v Daniel>Exactly. If the tech is just okay, it destroys the old task but starves the economy of the fuel needed to create the new task. Without that wealth generation, the reinstatement effect just stalls out completely.</v>

69
00:07:10.117 --> 00:07:22.021
<v Maya>Okay, so to prove this isn't just some modern theoretical thought experiment, the 2019 paper actually does the math on the last 150 years of American economic history. Let's look at that farming example I brought up earlier.</v>

70
00:07:22.481 --> 00:07:28.243
<v Daniel>Right, the 19th century agricultural mechanization. It's the textbook case of the reinstatement effect saving the day.</v>

71
00:07:28.523 --> 00:07:30.104
<v Maya>From the reapers and tractors?</v>

72
00:07:30.464 --> 00:07:36.615
<v Daniel>Yeah. From 1850 to 1910, the share of labor in farming plummeted from 33% to 17%.</v>

73
00:07:36.935 --> 00:07:38.056
<v Maya>That's a huge drop.</v>

74
00:07:38.296 --> 00:07:40.899
<v Daniel>It's a massive economy-rattling displacement effect.</v>

75
00:07:41.319 --> 00:07:46.544
<v Maya>But the industrial labor share simultaneously rose from 47% to 55%, right? That's</v>

76
00:07:46.584 --> 00:07:51.308
<v Daniel>because humans invented entirely new factory and clerical jobs. The tug-of-war balanced out.</v>

77
00:07:51.825 --> 00:08:06.770
<v Maya>So let's fast forward to modern times. The authors broke down the US wage bill into two distinct eras. I want you to kind of walk me through the difference between these two eras, but without drowning us in every single decimal point. What actually happened to the tug of</v>

78
00:08:06.790 --> 00:08:06.990
<v Maya>war?</v>

79
00:08:07.270 --> 00:08:22.065
<v Daniel>Fair enough. If we look at the delta, the change between the two eras, it tells the whole story. First, we had the golden era from 1947 to 1987. During this time, the wage bill grew about 2.5%</v>

80
00:08:22.425 --> 00:08:34.048
<v Daniel>a year. The jobs lost to machines were almost perfectly replaced by the invention of new human tasks. It was basically a one-to-one tie. Real wages grew healthily because productivity was booming.</v>

81
00:08:34.708 --> 00:08:38.529
<v Maya>But then we hit the stagnation era from 1987 to 2017. Right. And the rope snapped.</v>

82
00:08:41.272 --> 00:08:55.846
<v Daniel>The rope completely snapped. After 1987, wage bill growth fell to 1.3 percent and pretty much flatlined after 2000. Wow. The speed at which machines displaced humans essentially accelerated to 0.7 percent a year,</v>

83
00:08:56.467 --> 00:09:01.552
<v Daniel>while our ability to invent new tasks for humans slowed down to 0.35 percent.</v>

84
00:09:01.752 --> 00:09:02.893
<v Maya>So they weren't matching anymore.</v>

85
00:09:03.073 --> 00:09:09.972
<v Daniel>Not at all. The result is that the task content of the economy shifted against labor by about 10% cumulatively.</v>

86
00:09:10.393 --> 00:09:11.154
<v Maya>That's significant.</v>

87
00:09:11.534 --> 00:09:17.582
<v Daniel>And if you isolate just the manufacturing sector during that window, it was a brutal 30% shift against human labor.</v>

88
00:09:17.911 --> 00:09:32.640
<v Maya>OK, I have to play skeptic here for a second because a huge chunk of American manufacturing went overseas between 1987 and 2017. True. So how do the authors know this brutal 30 percent shift against labor is actually because of automation displacing workers and not just,</v>

89
00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:35.242
<v Maya>you know, the result of globalization and offshoring?</v>

90
00:09:35.909 --> 00:09:48.376
<v Daniel>It is a critical distinction, and the authors explicitly test for it. They found that while offshoring certainly happened, the specific mathematical signature of these labor share declines mapped directly onto the industry's adopting automation.</v>

91
00:09:48.896 --> 00:09:51.377
<v Maya>Like specifically where they brought in robots.</v>

92
00:09:52.037 --> 00:10:02.423
<v Daniel>Exactly. Industrial robots, specialized software, routine job shares. It tracks perfectly. And they point to a very evidence-based, policy-driven explanation for why this imbalance happened in the U.S.</v>

93
00:10:02.903 --> 00:10:12.468
<v Maya>Right. The mechanism behind the snapped rope. They point out that the U.S. tax code heavily subsidizes the purchase of equipment while heavily taxing the employment of human labor. It's</v>

94
00:10:12.508 --> 00:10:13.129
<v Daniel>a huge factor.</v>

95
00:10:13.549 --> 00:10:27.617
<v Maya>Plus, you have big tech business models that are inherently built on massive automation with tiny workforces. You add in declining public support for fundamental research and you get this ecosystem that artificially incentivizes replacing humans rather than augmenting them.</v>

96
00:10:28.114 --> 00:10:36.498
<v Daniel>The underlying takeaway there is that the direction of technology isn't an unstoppable force of nature. It's heavily influenced by how we structure our taxes and subsidies.</v>

97
00:10:36.958 --> 00:10:45.502
<v Maya>So that brings us to the 2020s. The 2019 paper set the stage, but how has all this held up against the absolute explosion of AI?</v>

98
00:10:45.522 --> 00:10:46.942
<v Daniel>Let's see.</v>

99
00:10:47.603 --> 00:10:57.507
<v Maya>Let's stress test it against the three major follow-up studies in our stack. I want to start with the 2024 study by David Otter and his colleagues that looked at eight decades of census data.</v>

100
00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:10.346
<v Daniel>Oh, the New Frontiers paper? That one provides incredible direct causal proof of the reinstatement effect. They found that roughly 60% of all employment in 2018 was in job titles that did not even exist in</v>

101
00:11:10.386 --> 00:11:17.249
<v Maya>1940. 60% of modern jobs are entirely invented tasks. That's just staggering.</v>

102
00:11:17.409 --> 00:11:26.393
<v Daniel>It really is. But they also found a darker trend. From 1940 to 1980, the new work we invented was mostly middle-paid production and clerical jobs.</v>

103
00:11:26.753 --> 00:11:27.853
<v Maya>Which built the middle class.</v>

104
00:11:28.153 --> 00:11:42.758
<v Daniel>Exactly. But after 1980, new work bifurcated. We started heavily inventing high-paid professional jobs and low-paid service jobs, essentially hollowing out the middle class. The erosive effect of automation really intensified.</v>

105
00:11:43.137 --> 00:11:57.744
<v Maya>Which leads right into Acemoglu and Restrepo's 2022 Econometrica paper. They found that automation explains a massive 50 to 70 percent of the change in the U.S. wage structure since 1980, mostly hitting those routine middle skill tasks.</v>

106
00:11:57.904 --> 00:12:05.468
<v Daniel>Right. It's the hard mathematical proof of what happens when displacement outpaces reinstatement. Inequality skyrockets.</v>

107
00:12:05.803 --> 00:12:10.985
<v Maya>And that perfectly sets up the most recent and honestly, perhaps most fiercely debated paper in our</v>

108
00:12:11.025 --> 00:12:13.606
<v Daniel>stack. The 2025 Asimoglu paper? Yeah.</v>

109
00:12:14.027 --> 00:12:28.853
<v Maya>The Simple Macroeconomics of AI. He applies this exact task-based framework to generative AI. And he estimates that about 20% of U.S. labor tasks are directly exposed to AI right now. Right. Okay. If 20% of all tasks are exposed,</v>

110
00:12:29.113 --> 00:12:32.555
<v Maya>the productivity gains must be absolutely astronomical, right?</v>

111
00:12:33.133 --> 00:12:47.343
<v Daniel>This is where the academic debate gets incredibly heated. He calculates that the total factor productivity or TFP gains over the next 10 years will be shockingly modest. How modest? He estimates no more than</v>

112
00:12:47.363 --> 00:12:56.310
<v Daniel>0.66%. And he argues it might be under 0.53% when you factor in what he calls hard to learn tasks.</v>

113
00:12:57.127 --> 00:13:09.379
<v Maya>Wait, wait, wait. Anyone who has used ChatGBT to write a report in 10 seconds is going to aggressively disagree with that number. How can the economic impact possibly be that small when the micro level speed is so obvious?</v>

114
00:13:09.619 --> 00:13:20.389
<v Daniel>I know, it sounds wrong. But to understand why, you have to look at the mechanism of how work actually gets done. It comes down to the difference between easy to learn tasks and hard to learn tasks.</v>

115
00:13:20.729 --> 00:13:21.170
<v Maya>Okay.</v>

116
00:13:21.633 --> 00:13:31.940
<v Daniel>Yes, AI can write a standard report easily because there are clear metrics of success and billions of examples on the internet to train on. But a massive amount of human work is deeply contextual.</v>

117
00:13:32.521 --> 00:13:32.901
<v Maya>Like what?</v>

118
00:13:33.661 --> 00:13:47.868
<v Daniel>diagnosing a patient with multiple overlapping rare diseases or negotiating a complex corporate merger. These rely on judgment, reading the room, unwritten cultural rules. AI struggles there. Furthermore, he points out a</v>

119
00:13:47.908 --> 00:13:53.771
<v Daniel>macroeconomic drag. Some new tasks created by AI have negative social value.</v>

120
00:13:54.372 --> 00:13:59.094
<v Maya>Wait, how does a negative social value task drag down macroeconomic productivity?</v>

121
00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:13.826
<v Daniel>Think about where human capital goes. If top engineering talent has paid millions of dollars to design algorithms for online manipulation, or to create undetectable deepfakes, or just to build software to bypass another company's AI filters.</v>

122
00:14:14.747 --> 00:14:17.528
<v Maya>Oh, I see. They are not solving physical world problems.</v>

123
00:14:17.728 --> 00:14:29.573
<v Daniel>Exactly. They aren't curing diseases or building better infrastructure. That actively diverts brilliant human capital away from true economic productivity, dragging down that overall TFP number.</v>

124
00:14:29.862 --> 00:14:42.435
<v Maya>OK, that makes a lot of sense. So let's synthesize all of this. Let's build a really clear bottom line scorecard for you. What did the classic 2019 paper get right? What is highly debated today and what's the ultimate takeaway?</v>

125
00:14:42.735 --> 00:14:57.707
<v Daniel>OK, well, what the 2019 paper absolutely nailed is the accounting method. Measuring displacement against reinstatement is definitively the most accurate way to understand technology's impact on labor. Right. Furthermore, they proved that inventing</v>

126
00:14:57.827 --> 00:15:07.154
<v Daniel>new work is the historical engine of human prosperity. And their warning about so-so automation being an economic drag has proven incredibly prescient.</v>

127
00:15:07.622 --> 00:15:11.564
<v Maya>But looking at the 2026 evidence, things definitely got complicated.</v>

128
00:15:11.745 --> 00:15:11.925
<v Daniel>Very</v>

129
00:15:11.965 --> 00:15:26.694
<v Maya>much so. For one, generative AI is often an augmentation technology at the individual task level. Unlike a physical robot that just takes a physical task, a single AI tool can displace a task and reinstate a completely different analytical task for the</v>

130
00:15:26.734 --> 00:15:28.635
<v Maya>exact same worker simultaneously.</v>

131
00:15:29.035 --> 00:15:30.156
<v Daniel>Yeah, the lines are blurring.</v>

132
00:15:30.575 --> 00:15:38.597
<v Maya>Also, the 2019 math inferred the existence of tasks indirectly by looking at labor shares rather than, you know, seeing the new tasks directly.</v>

133
00:15:39.177 --> 00:15:48.100
<v Daniel>Yes, the methodology is always evolving. To make this highly actionable, let's provide three specific quotable findings from this stack along with our best counter arguments.</v>

134
00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:49.000
<v Maya>Let's do it.</v>

135
00:15:49.980 --> 00:15:55.342
<v Daniel>Finding number one, 60% of 2018 employment is in jobs that didn't exist in 1940.</v>

136
00:15:56.130 --> 00:16:08.753
<v Maya>OK, but looking at that data, I have to play devil's advocate. Couldn't a skeptic just argue this is title inflation? Like we just rebranded secretary to administrative professional rather than genuinely creating new economic tasks.</v>

137
00:16:09.234 --> 00:16:17.836
<v Daniel>That is the primary objection. Yes. Finding number two. Automation explains 50 to 70 percent of U.S. wage structure changes since 1980.</v>

138
00:16:18.720 --> 00:16:31.689
<v Maya>And the strongest objection there is something called monopsony power, right? Which basically means giant corporations have so much market dominance that they can artificially suppress wages regardless of what the technology is doing.</v>

139
00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:38.894
<v Daniel>Right. And the authors do acknowledge corporate markups play a role. So the debate on the exact percentage of blame continues.</v>

140
00:16:39.295 --> 00:16:40.295
<v Maya>Okay. Finding number three.</v>

141
00:16:40.556 --> 00:16:45.199
<v Daniel>Finding number three. AI will boost productivity by less than 0.66% over 10 years.</v>

142
00:16:47.461 --> 00:17:02.105
<v Maya>And the objection here is that early task level studies show much faster adoption and massive efficiency gains. Plus, Asimoglu's low estimate basically ignores the completely new tasks that AI will inevitably invent, which could just</v>

143
00:17:02.305 --> 00:17:03.646
<v Maya>supercharge productivity later.</v>

144
00:17:03.666 --> 00:17:18.057
<v Daniel>That's very possible. But if we pull back from the specific percentages... The single organizing idea that survives all of this stress testing is this. The future of work depends entirely on the direction of</v>

145
00:17:18.117 --> 00:17:22.020
<v Daniel>technology. It is a choice, not an unstoppable force.</v>

146
00:17:22.358 --> 00:17:35.107
<v Maya>I used a pretty weak metaphor earlier about a train, but let's reframe it using the mechanisms we just discussed. Right now, our tax code treats human workers like a luxury good, and it treats machines like a tax write-off.</v>

147
00:17:35.207 --> 00:17:35.687
<v Daniel>Really does.</v>

148
00:17:36.348 --> 00:17:50.478
<v Maya>We aren't helpless passengers tied to the AI train tracks. We literally built the tracks. We can just as easily choose to subsidize human augmentation. Technology isn't a train on a fixed track that we're tied to. It's a car, and society is holding the steering wheel.</v>

149
00:17:50.739 --> 00:18:02.985
<v Daniel>which brings us to a massive practical challenge. If we are going to steer toward new tasks, and new tasks are the only way to save human labor, someone actually has to invent, incubate, and teach those tasks.</v>

150
00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:18.128
<v Maya>Which lands squarely on the doorstep of higher education. When you look at this macroeconomic data through the lens of a university, the mandate changes completely. Institutions can't just teach students how to defend themselves against automation by learning some basic coding.</v>

151
00:18:18.248 --> 00:18:19.228
<v Daniel>No, that's not enough anymore.</v>

152
00:18:19.589 --> 00:18:22.390
<v Maya>They have to teach the skills required for the new work.</v>

153
00:18:22.610 --> 00:18:29.594
<v Daniel>Yes. Universities are effectively the engines of the reinstatement effect. But that creates a massive hurdle.</v>

154
00:18:30.152 --> 00:18:44.842
<v Maya>Right, the credentialing problem. Think about the administrators and curriculum designers you might work with. How do they realistically write a syllabus for a job title that won't even exist for five years? If 60% of future jobs haven't been invented yet, what actually goes</v>

155
00:18:45.102 --> 00:18:46.142
<v Maya>on the diploma?</v>

156
00:18:46.663 --> 00:18:55.628
<v Daniel>It requires a fundamental shift in how we view education. Higher ed needs to partner directly with employers to focus on augmenting workers rather than replacing them.</v>

157
00:18:55.869 --> 00:18:57.009
<v Maya>So less memorization.</v>

158
00:18:57.370 --> 00:19:12.073
<v Daniel>Exactly. The curriculum has to shift away from rote knowledge transfer, which AI can do instantly, toward adaptability, complex problem solving, and contextual judgment. You have to teach those exact, hard-to-learn tasks that Achamoglu pointed out</v>

159
00:19:12.173 --> 00:19:13.053
<v Daniel>AI struggles with.</v>

160
00:19:13.454 --> 00:19:24.361
<v Maya>We also have to talk about the exposure risk here. When displacement outpaces reinstatement, like we saw from 1987 to 2017, the economic pain isn't shared equally.</v>

161
00:19:24.661 --> 00:19:25.081
<v Daniel>Not at all.</v>

162
00:19:25.301 --> 00:19:40.231
<v Maya>The data shows that automation hits middle skill and entry-level routine tasks the absolute hardest. The most vulnerable populations are early career workers, adult learners, and first-generation students. Explicitly, urban public university</v>

163
00:19:40.292 --> 00:19:46.676
<v Maya>systems with student bodies just like CUNY's in New York City are standing right on the front lines of this economic shift.</v>

164
00:19:46.756 --> 00:19:55.803
<v Daniel>They absolutely are. If a university is training first-generation students for routine cognitive jobs today, they are walking them right into a displacement buzzsaw.</v>

165
00:19:56.203 --> 00:20:00.944
<v Maya>So how do we fix it? How do we prepare those specific students for this landscape?</v>

166
00:20:01.124 --> 00:20:05.825
<v Daniel>You don't teach the job title. You teach the capacity for task recombination.</v>

167
00:20:06.125 --> 00:20:08.906
<v Maya>Task recombination. Okay.</v>

168
00:20:09.186 --> 00:20:23.689
<v Daniel>You train students to use AI to aggressively automate their own routine tasks. By doing that, they free up their own cognitive space to specialize in the highly contextual, interpersonal, and strategic tasks that machines cannot replicate.</v>

169
00:20:24.119 --> 00:20:25.880
<v Maya>So they build their own job security.</v>

170
00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:30.562
<v Daniel>Exactly. You teach the student how to be the creator of their own reinstatement effect.</v>

171
00:20:31.082 --> 00:20:41.907
<v Maya>That is a massive paradigm shift for how we think about a degree. So let's bring this all together. The popular narrative that robots will take all the jobs is just dangerously incomplete.</v>

172
00:20:42.108 --> 00:20:43.268
<v Daniel>It misses half the equation.</v>

173
00:20:43.568 --> 00:20:53.373
<v Maya>Right. It misses entirely half of the macroeconomic equation. The real danger isn't that machines do old work. The danger is that we fail to invent the new jobs.</v>

174
00:20:53.867 --> 00:20:58.378
<v Daniel>We have to actively design an economy that demands new human tasks.</v>

175
00:20:59.196 --> 00:21:13.906
<v Maya>Which leaves us with a final provocative thought to mull over on your own. Right now, as we've established, our corporate structures and tax codes heavily incentivize companies to buy machines instead of hiring humans. We are literally subsidizing the displacement effect.</v>

176
00:21:14.266 --> 00:21:14.546
<v Daniel>We are.</v>

177
00:21:15.067 --> 00:21:24.833
<v Maya>But what would happen if we designed an economic policy that actively rewarded businesses for inventing entirely new tasks for human beings? What would that economy look like?</v>

178
00:21:25.332 --> 00:21:34.763
<v Daniel>It's a profound question. It shifts the entire burden from the individual worker desperately defending their job to society actively incentivizing human potential.</v>

179
00:21:35.403 --> 00:21:47.197
<v Maya>We'd finally be drawing a new blueprint, one that actually works in the real world. Thank you for bringing this stack of research to the table and for joining us on this custom deep dive. Keep steering the car.</v>
